#: processes/copy-out-materialized.bpmn:126
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "`check:materialized-fixity`. Run against the SOURCE, not the copy — the copy is new content with no claim to verify, and the source is the thing a botched copy-out would have disturbed. A mismatch here means the copy-out was an edit in place wearing a copy's clothes, and it is the only signal that tells the two apart."
msgstr ""

#: processes/copy-out-materialized.bpmn:95
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "A read, and the only interaction this process has with the source. Its digest and `provenance` are carried forward: the digest so Task_VerifySourceUntouched has something to check against, and `provenance` so the copy can name what it came out of without re-deriving it."
msgstr ""

#: processes/copy-out-materialized.bpmn:134
#. Diagram label
msgid "A working copy, saying what it is a copy of"
msgstr ""

#: processes/copy-out-materialized.bpmn:44
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "ANY reader, and that is a ruling rather than an oversight — the owner, 2026-09-22, chose it over restricting the copy-out to an editor. Refusing a copy would not protect the original, because the original is protected by being read-only; a permission here would only stop people working. So this lane has no entry condition, and the one decision in it is about the TARGET rather than about the actor."
msgstr ""

#: processes/copy-out-materialized.bpmn:63
#. Diagram element documentation
msgid "Asks the question the whole process exists to keep answerable: does the SOURCE still hash to its recorded digest? A copy-out that disturbed the original is not a copy-out, and this lane is the only thing that can tell the difference after the fact — the bytes look the same either way."
msgstr ""

